    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "A structure contour map from 3D seismic data including a full graben is compared to the calculated displacements from geomechanical modeling. The good correspondence between observed and calculated displacements suggests that the modeling technique can be applied to specific fault geometries to better constrain the shape and slip distribution on faults. Given this information about fault geometry, the local stress field can be computed and used to make predictions about subseismic deformation. The effects of fault dimensions and slip distributions on vertical displacement in the volume containing a simple isolated fault are considered. These relationships demonstrate how structure contours are more sensitive to fault height than length and that the vertical displacement field around a fault is affected by the slip distribution.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Young, Scott S., Davies, Russel K. and Pollard, David D. (1997). Structure Contours and Displacement Fields Around a Full Graben: A Comparison Between Seismic Data and Geomechanical Models. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at: http://purl.stanford.edu/xs794ky7666",
